+# Batch Writes and Run Report Implementation Plan
+
+> **For agentic workers:** REQUIRED SUB-SKILL: Use superpowers:subagent-driven-development (recommended) or superpowers:executing-plans to implement this plan task-by-task. Steps use checkbox (`- [ ]`) syntax for tracking.
+
+**Goal:** Make the export job write in `put_many` batches of at most 25 while keeping the request budget and the cursor exact, and replace the placeholder `exporter.report` with the command-line run report.
+
+**Architecture:** `exporter/job.py` keeps one `run` function. It now holds the records it has read but not yet dealt with in a `pending` list (always starting with a live record) and writes them with one `put_many` when 25 live records have gathered or the source runs out. A small `_write` helper checks the budget before each request and, only when the write happens, counts the pending records and moves the cursor past them. `exporter/report.py` validates its arguments and the records file, runs one export against a fresh in-memory `Store`, and prints the one-line report.
+
+**Tech Stack:** Python 3 standard library only (`dataclasses`, `json`), `unittest`, `unittest.mock`.
+
+**Spec:** `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-01-08-exporter-design.md` (sections 2.2, 3.1, 3.2, 3.3 and 4). This plan follows `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-01-10-export-job.md`, which built the source and store stand-ins and a one-`put`-per-record `run`.
+
+## Global Constraints
+
+- Tests run with `python3 -m unittest` from the repository root; no third-party packages.
+- The exporter writes with `put_many`, in batches of at most 25 (`exporter.store.MAX_BATCH`). It does not call `put`.
+- A larger batch is refused by the store and nothing in it is written, so the job must never send more than 25.
+- The job's page-size constant is `SOURCE_PAGE_SIZE`, so it cannot be confused with the store's batch limit. `PAGE_SIZE` goes away.
+- A run makes at most `MAX_REQUESTS` (500) store requests. It checks the budget before every request, and stops rather than exceed it, recording that it stopped.
+- A run returns a cursor: the id of the last record it has dealt with, whether written or skipped. The next run starts after it, so a run that stops at the budget loses nothing and repeats nothing.
+- A record whose status is `draft` is counted as skipped and not written.
+- `python3 -m exporter.report RECORDS_JSON [CURSOR]` prints exactly one line: `exported=N skipped=N requests=N stopped_by_budget=yes|no cursor=ID`
+- It exits 0 when the run completed or stopped at the budget, and 2 on a usage error.
+
+## Decisions this plan makes where the spec is silent
+
+- **A draft is "dealt with" only when every record before it is.** A draft read while a batch is still unwritten is held with that batch. If the budget stops the run before the batch is written, the held drafts are neither counted as skipped nor passed by the cursor; the next run counts them once. Without this, the cursor would have to jump over unwritten records (losing them) or the skipped count would repeat across runs.
+- **The budget is per run.** `RunResult.requests` counts the requests this run made, and the budget is checked against it, not against `Store.requests` (which counts over the store's lifetime). `requests=N` in the report is this number.
+- **A bad records file is a usage error.** A missing or unreadable file, invalid JSON, a top level that is not an array, or a record that is not an object with a non-empty string `id` and a string `status` all exit 2 with one line on stderr and nothing on stdout. The spec defines only exit codes 0 and 2, and a traceback (exit 1) is not a report.
+- **An empty cursor prints as `cursor=`** (nothing after the `=`), which is what a run that dealt with no records and was given no cursor returns.
+
+## Review Focus
+
+1. **Drafts inside an unwritten batch at a budget stop** — the cursor must stay before the first unwritten live record and those drafts must not be counted yet, so repeated runs add up to exactly the live and draft totals. (Task 1: `test_drafts_held_with_an_unwritten_batch_are_not_dealt_with`, `test_a_stopped_run_resumes_losing_and_repeating_nothing`.)
+2. **Re-running with the final cursor, or over nothing but drafts** — makes no store request at all (no empty `put_many`), reports `requests=0`, and keeps the cursor. (Task 1: `test_makes_no_request_when_nothing_is_left_to_write`; Task 2: `test_reports_a_run_with_nothing_to_export`.)
+3. **Budget spent exactly as the work runs out** — is a completed run, `stopped_by_budget=no`; trailing drafts need no request, so they are skipped, not a reason to stop. (Task 1: `test_spending_the_budget_exactly_is_not_a_stop`.)
+4. **A malformed records file** (missing, not JSON, not an array, integer or missing ids, missing status) — exits 2 with a one-line message on stderr, not a traceback. Integer ids matter most: `Source` compares ids with `"" <`, which raises `TypeError` on an int. (Task 2: `test_a_bad_records_file_is_a_usage_error`.)
+5. **Batches that straddle source pages** — a batch fills across a page boundary instead of being flushed short at every page end, so 30 records at page size 10 take 2 requests, not 3. (Task 1: `test_batches_span_source_pages`.)
